  "Question": "If \\(x,\\) \\(y\\) and \\(y-\\frac1x\\) are not \\(0,\\) then \\[ \\frac{x-\\frac1y}{y-\\frac1x} \\] equals",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(1\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\frac{x}{y}\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\frac{y}{x}\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\frac{x}{y}-\\frac{y}{x}\\)",
    "E": "\\(xy-\\frac1{xy}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "Writing each difference as a single fraction reveals a common factor:\n\\[\nx-\\frac1y=\\frac{xy-1}{y},\\qquad y-\\frac1x=\\frac{xy-1}{x}.\n\\]\nThe assumptions give \\(x\\ne0\\), \\(y\\ne0\\), and \\(xy-1=x(y-1/x)\\ne0\\). Thus we may divide by these quantities and cancel the common factor:\n\\[\n\\frac{x-\\frac1y}{y-\\frac1x}\n=\\frac{xy-1}{y}\\cdot\\frac{x}{xy-1}\n=\\boxed{\\frac{x}{y}}.\n\\]\nThe answer is choice B.",
